## 3. Harness coverage is the reason two of the three hid

| family            | live differential harness?                    | breaks |
|-------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-------:|
| itx               | yes, `src/itx.rs` (`ITX_MISMATCH`)            |    293 |
| mc_put            | yes, `src/mc.rs` (`MC_PUT_MISMATCH`)          |      0 |
| mc_prep           | yes, `src/mc.rs` (`MC_PREP_MISMATCH`)         |     91 |
| **mc_other**      | **none — no harness exists**                  |**407** |
| cdef              | yes, `src/cdef.rs`                            |      0 |
| **loopfilter**    | **compiled but structurally dead** (below)    |      0 |
| looprestoration   | yes, `src/looprestoration.rs`                 |      0 |
| filmgrain         | none                                          |      0 |
| ipred             | n/a (no NEON in this build)                   |      0 |

**`mc_other`** is `avg` / `w_avg` / `mask` / `w_mask` / `blend` / `blend_dir` /
`w_mask` / `mc_scaled` / `mct_scaled` / `warp8x8` / `warp8x8t` / `emu_edge` /
`resize`. `src/mc.rs` dual-computes only `mc_put_direct` and `mct_prep_direct`.
The other twelve dispatchers have never been compared to scalar by anything.

**The aarch64 loop-filter harness cannot fire.**
`safe_simd::loopfilter_arm::loopfilter_sb_dispatch` **always returns `false`**
(deliberately — the NEON tier is wired one level down, at
`lf_compact_run_neon`, called from `LfBlock::filter_run` inside
`loop_filter_sb128_rust`). `src/loopfilter.rs`'s `__simd_test` block only runs
when `simd_handled` is true, so `LF_MISMATCH` is unreachable on aarch64 and the
"scalar reference" it would compare against is the NEON path itself. The loop
filter is not currently guilty — `only_loopfilter` breaks 0 — but that verdict
comes from the ablation, **not** from the harness, which supplies no evidence
either way. (There *is* real coverage elsewhere:
`src/loopfilter.rs::neon_parity` unit-tests `lf_compact_run_neon` against a
scalar oracle. Keep it; it is why the loop filter is clean.)

---

## 4. What is actually wrong, per family

### 4a. `mc_other` (407 vectors) — HIGHEST YIELD

Two independent defects, both confirmed by the new
`src::safe_simd::mc_arm::compound_parity` tests:

**(i) `avg_8bpc_inner`'s vector paths compute twice the correct average.**
`vqrdmulhq_n_s16(a, b)` is `sat((2·a·b + 2^15) >> 16)`. With `b = 2048` that is
`(a + 8) >> 4`, i.e. `sum/16`. The scalar reference is `(t1 + t2 + 16) >> 5`,
i.e. `sum/32`. **The multiplier should be 1024.** The kernel's own comment
derives `(a·1024 + 8192) >> 14` and then says "We want `(a·1024 + 16384) >> 15`"
— it names the discrepancy and ships anyway. The scalar tail below it is
correct, which is the fingerprint: `avg8` passes at 4x4 and 4x8 (tail only) and
fails at every width ≥ 8. Same `vqrdmulhq_n_s16(sum, 2048)` appears in
`w_avg_8bpc_inner`; there the *tail* is also off, in the other direction (it
divides by 512 where the reference divides by 256), so check both.

**(ii) `w_mask_{8,16}bpc_inner` add a `PREP_BIAS` that belongs to neither
convention.** Both write `rnd = (32 << intermediate_bits) + 8192 * 64`. At 8bpc
`BitDepth8::PREP_BIAS` is **0**, so the scalar `rnd` is 512 and the kernel's is
524,800 — the source comment "PREP_BIAS = 8192 for 8bpc in compound" is simply
false. At 16bpc its siblings add nothing (§4b), so it disagrees with them too.

**(iii) `intermediate_bits` is hardcoded to 4 in four 16bpc kernels** —
`avg_16bpc_inner` (mc_arm.rs:172), `w_avg_16bpc_inner` (:389),
`mask_16bpc_inner` (:606), `w_mask_16bpc_inner` (:2654). The spec value is
`14 - bitdepth`: 4 at 10-bit, **2 at 12-bit**. Correct at 10, wrong at 12 — the
exact shape of the CDEF `bitdepth_min_8` bug that hid for months.
`compound_16bpc_shift_tracks_bitdepth` measures the effective shift without
touching the additive constant and finds all three of avg/w_avg/mask off at both
10 and 12 bits; `w_mask_16bpc_segmentation_mask_matches_scalar` passes at 10-bit
and fails at 12-bit, which is the clean isolated signature of this term
(`mask_sh = bitdepth + intermediate_bits - 4` → 12 instead of 10).

### 4b. `mc_prep` (91 vectors, 10 + 12 bit) — and a seam decision it forces

`prep_8tap_16bpc_inner` (mc_arm.rs:4489) hardcodes `intermediate_bits = 4u8`
while its `put` twin two hundred lines up derives it correctly
(`bitdepth_max.leading_zeros() - 2`, mc_arm.rs:4250). `CONTEXT-HANDOFF.md` §3
flagged this as latent in 2026-06; it is live.

But the dominant `MC_PREP_MISMATCH max_diff` is **8192 = `BitDepth16::PREP_BIAS`,
and it is 8192 at 10-bit too**, where the hardcoded 4 is right. So there is a
second, larger divergence: **the aarch64 16bpc prep does not subtract the prep
bias.** `src/mc.rs`'s `prep_8tap_rust` ends every output with
`.sub_prep_bias::<BD>()`; this kernel does not. And `avg_16bpc_inner` /
`w_avg_16bpc_inner` / `mask_16bpc_inner` correspondingly do **not** add it back
inside `rnd`, so those four are internally paired on an unbiased convention that
the scalar reference does not share.

**This is a decision the fix has to make before touching a line**, and it is why
all 91 mc_prep vectors also need mc_other: either
(a) make `prep_8tap_16bpc_inner` scalar-conventional and add `PREP_BIAS·k` back
in avg/w_avg/mask (and *remove* the bogus one from w_mask at 8bpc), or
(b) keep the unbiased convention and strip the bias from `w_mask_{8,16}` — but
then the arm prep can never be compared to the scalar prep, and
`MC_PREP_MISMATCH` becomes permanently noisy and must be retired or rebiased.
(a) is the smaller blast radius and keeps the harness meaningful.

Same trap applies to any 16bpc value test written against a scalar-convention
oracle: it will report one `PREP_BIAS` of difference that is a seam artefact, not
the defect. `compound_parity` is deliberately convention-free at 16bpc
(shift-only + the bias-invariant segmentation mask) and full-value only at 8bpc,
where `PREP_BIAS` is 0 and `prep_8tap_8bpc_inner` is independently known
bit-exact (zero `MC_PREP_MISMATCH` across all 358 8-bit/data vectors).

### 4c. `itx` (293 vectors, all 8bpc)

Four cells, from `__simd_test_log`:

| size  | type            | calls | vectors |
|-------|-----------------|------:|--------:|
| 16x16 | 11 = `H_DCT`    | 7,190 |     243 |
| 16x32 | 9 = `IDTX`      |   142 |      51 |
| 32x16 | 9 = `IDTX`      |    78 |      47 |
| 16x32 | 0 = `DCT_DCT`   |    16 |      13 |

`H_DCT` at 16x16 dominates. Per the dispatch table, `H_DCT` routes to
`inv_txfm_add_dct_identity_16x16_8bpc_neon_inner`. The IDTX rect2 pair is the
`CONTEXT-HANDOFF.md` recipe's open item (16x32/32x16 need the ×1/√2
`scale_input` the NEON omits; rect2 applies iff `log2(w)+log2(h)` is odd).

---

## 5. `bitdepth_min_8` audit (the CDEF-bug-shape sweep)

Every `bitdepth_min_8` use in every `*_arm.rs`, against its scalar and x86 twin:

* `cdef_arm.rs` (31 uses) — **clean**. `filter_params` has the #446 fix
  (`4 - (pri_strength >> bitdepth_min_8 & 1)`); `bitdepth_min_8_of`, the
  `neg_shift` splats and the direction search all derive from `bitdepth_max`.
  Ablation agrees: cdef breaks 0.
* `loopfilter_arm.rs` (8 uses) — **clean**. `f`, `clip_hi`, and the per-group
  `e`/`i`/`h` shifts match `src/loopfilter.rs::loop_filter` term for term; the
  narrow filter's `f1`/`f2`/`f3` and both arithmetic shifts match.
* `looprestoration_arm.rs` (3 uses) — **clean**, and the two literal-looking
  derivations (`if bitdepth_max == 1023 { 2 } else { 4 }`) are inside 16bpc-only
  functions where 10 and 12 are the only inputs.
* `filmgrain_arm.rs` (14 uses) — same 16bpc-only literal pattern
  (`if bitdepth_max >= 4095 { 4 } else { 2 }`), consistent with
  `src/filmgrain.rs`'s `bd.bitdepth() - 8`. Ablation agrees: 0.
* `mc_arm.rs`, `itx_arm*.rs` — do not use `bitdepth_min_8`; they use
  `intermediate_bits`, **and that is where the live instance of this bug class
  is** (§4a-iii, §4b). Five hardcoded `intermediate_bits = 4` in 16bpc kernels.

So: the sweep found no *new* `bitdepth_min_8` sibling, and the same bug *shape*
one identifier over, five times.

---

## 6. "NEON tier is actually scalar" recount

Real aarch64 intrinsic call sites per module at this base (regex over the
`vld|vst|vadd|…` families, not the loose `v\w+_\w+\(`):

```
mc_arm.rs                937      itx_arm_neon_rect_large.rs  190
loopfilter_arm.rs        223      itx_arm_neon_rect.rs        171
cdef_arm.rs              104      itx_arm_neon_16x16.rs       146
filmgrain_arm.rs          64      itx_arm_neon_32.rs          130
ipred_arm.rs              48      itx_arm_neon_8x8.rs         128
                                  itx_arm_neon_{64,large_rect} 92 each
itx_arm.rs                 0  (dispatch only; kernels live in itx_arm_neon_*)
looprestoration_arm.rs     0  ← still the scalar reference wearing a NEON name
refmvs_arm.rs              2  (asm-only, not in this build)
```

`ROADMAP_SIMD_PORTING.md` R3's three zero-count files are down to one: CDEF and
the loop filter have since been ported. **`looprestoration_arm.rs` is 1,531
lines of scalar with `use core::arch::aarch64::*` at the top**, dispatched
unconditionally (`lr_filter_dispatch` ends in `true`) at both bit depths. It is
a hand-written *duplicate* of `src/looprestoration.rs` — any drift between them
is a bug by construction, and it currently costs a real perf tier. It is not
guilty of any of the 464 (ablation: 0), so it is a perf/dedup item, not a
correctness one. `ipred_arm.rs`'s 48 intrinsics are all inside
`#[cfg(feature = "asm")] mod ffi` and unreachable here.

